WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.140
- This is the Uffington White Horse

00:00:02.150 --> 00:00:05.020
and it is somewhere
around 3,000 years old.

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:06.730
It was carved into this
hill in Oxfordshire

00:00:06.730 --> 00:00:08.610
before the British isles
had written history,

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:11.910
so we have no idea who made it, or why.

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:14.570
And until humanity invented
aircraft millennia later,

00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:16.520
no one could even really see it properly.

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:18.600
The view from the ground here isn't great.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:20.420
We're also not allowed
to fly a drone over it,

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:22.120
so you're going to have to make
do with a satellite photo

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:23.440
to see the whole thing.

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:26.990
This is a 3,000 year old piece of art.

00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:30.513
And I am now going to smash
part of it with this hammer.

00:00:32.690 --> 00:00:34.890
- We're smashing the chalk
into the figure of the Horse today

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:37.940
to preserve its appearance
and keep its longevity

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:40.130
as people have done for
the last 3,000 years,

00:00:40.130 --> 00:00:41.280
otherwise it wouldn't be here.

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:43.620
We're taking new chalk from
the quarry just behind me,

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:46.440
about 50 metres away,
10 tonnes in all.

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:49.830
And yes, that's being applied to the Horse
by the use of a club hammer.

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:51.640
Before that though, the
Horse is weeded,

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:53.740
or "scoured" for the old traditional term,

00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:55.630
so we can get on to the best surface.

00:00:55.630 --> 00:00:58.470
We rechalk and scour the
Horse every year now.

00:00:58.470 --> 00:01:02.060
They're all volunteers. Previous years
have been years of neglect,

00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:05.283
maybe 20, 25 years,
but traditionally,

00:01:05.283 --> 00:01:07.110
back in Victorian times every seven years.

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:08.537
But we're proud of the Horse here,

00:01:08.537 --> 00:01:11.280
and we want to keep it
looking its best as long as we can.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:13.780
Oxford University,
back in the 1990s,

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:17.160
came out with a newly developed sensor back then,

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:19.890
which was able to determine
the last time sunlight

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:23.350
touched the bottom of the trenches
on which the Horse is founded.

00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:27.430
And that put it to what we
know today, 3,000 years ago.

00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:30.370
This is by far the oldest
chalk figure in the country,

00:01:30.370 --> 00:01:31.900
nothing else comes anywhere near it.

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:34.650
We don't know for sure about
how the Horse was put in,

00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:36.360
or for what reason.

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:38.220
We imagine that there might
have been a wooden tower

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:41.410
constructed from which the
seer would be able to direct

00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:44.070
"left, right, missed a bit,"
that kind of thing.

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:46.701
Part of the mystery or the
enigma of the White Horse Hill

00:01:46.701 --> 00:01:49.500
is what makes it so
appealing to people that come.

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:51.140
You can use your own imagination.

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:52.890
What would happen if we
didn't chalk the Horse?

00:01:52.890 --> 00:01:55.660
I think it would grow
over within 20 or 30 years.

00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:57.740
That's what we say to the
people who've been kind enough

00:01:57.740 --> 00:01:59.070
to come along today and help us;

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:01.840
is they're actually contributing
to living history here.

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:04.557
Without that human intervention,
it would grass over

00:02:04.557 --> 00:02:07.333
25, 30 years, and be lost forever.

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:10.300
- This is art that's been preserved

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:12.820
by generation after generation after
generation after generation.

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:15.500
Repair and upkeep that's
been going on for millennia.

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:17.530
Through the Roman empire,
the medieval period,

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:19.230
a lot of invasions, the English Civil War,

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:20.860
the formation of the United Kingdom;

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:22.900
all the way up
to the Information Age.

00:02:22.910 --> 00:02:28.390
Every so often, groups of people have
come together on this hill, to preserve this.

00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:31.980
It is a very real connection with the folks
who came before us.

00:02:44.890 --> 00:02:46.340
Smashed it.

00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:49.153
Smashed it!
...nothing, just nothing.

